Implement a generic `MinHeap<T>` class in TypeScript in file `heap.ts`.

## Required API

```ts
class MinHeap<T> {
  constructor(comparator: (a: T, b: T) => number);
  push(item: T): void;      // O(log n)
  pop(): T | undefined;     // O(log n) — removes and returns min
  peek(): T | undefined;    // O(1) — returns min without removing
  get size(): number;       // O(1)
  toSortedArray(): T[];     // returns elements in sorted order (non-destructive)
}
```

## Required test cases (must pass with `ts-node heap.ts`)

```ts
// Test 1: numbers
const h = new MinHeap<number>((a, b) => a - b);
[5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2].forEach(n => h.push(n));
console.assert(h.peek() === 1, "peek should be 1");
console.assert(h.pop() === 1, "first pop should be 1");
console.assert(h.pop() === 2, "second pop should be 2");
console.assert(h.size === 4, "size should be 4 after 2 pops");

// Test 2: objects by priority field
type Task = { name: string; priority: number };
const taskHeap = new MinHeap<Task>((a, b) => a.priority - b.priority);
taskHeap.push({ name: "low", priority: 10 });
taskHeap.push({ name: "high", priority: 1 });
taskHeap.push({ name: "mid", priority: 5 });
console.assert(taskHeap.pop()?.name === "high", "should pop highest priority task");

// Test 3: toSortedArray
const h2 = new MinHeap<number>((a, b) => a - b);
[4, 2, 7, 1].forEach(n => h2.push(n));
const sorted = h2.toSortedArray();
console.assert(JSON.stringify(sorted) === '[1,2,4,7]', "toSortedArray failed");
console.assert(h2.size === 4, "toSortedArray should be non-destructive");
```

## Constraints
- Pure TypeScript, no external libraries
- Compiles with `tsc --strict heap.ts`
- Internal storage must be an array (array-backed binary heap)
- Max 100 lines

## Acceptance criteria
- All 3 test groups pass when run with `ts-node heap.ts`
- No import of external libraries

Implement DependencyResolver and CyclicDependencyError in dep_resolver.js as ES module. Topological sort via DFS with grey/black cycle detection. resolve() and resolveAll() return packages in installation order. No duplicates.
Write `dep_resolver.ts` (or `dep_resolver.js` as ESM) implementing a dependency resolver.

## Interface
```typescript
type DependencyGraph = Record<string, string[]>;
// key = package name, value = list of direct dependencies

class DependencyResolver {
  constructor(graph: DependencyGraph);

  resolve(entryPoint: string): string[];
  // Returns packages in installation order (topological sort):
  // dependencies before dependents.
  // Throws Error if entryPoint not in graph.
  // Throws CyclicDependencyError (extends Error) if cycle detected.

  resolveAll(): string[];
  // Resolve entire graph. Returns topo-sorted list of all packages.
  // Throws CyclicDependencyError if any cycle detected.
}

class CyclicDependencyError extends Error {
  cycle: string[]; // e.g. ["a", "b", "c", "a"]
}
```

## Algorithm
- Use DFS with grey/black coloring to detect cycles
- Grey = currently in DFS stack (cycle detected if revisited)
- Black = fully processed
- Output order: a package appears after all its dependencies

## Test assertions
```typescript
import { DependencyResolver, CyclicDependencyError } from './dep_resolver.js';

// Simple chain: c -> b -> a (a has no deps)
const g1 = { a: [], b: ["a"], c: ["b"] };
const r1 = new DependencyResolver(g1);
const order1 = r1.resolve("c");
const aIdx = order1.indexOf("a"), bIdx = order1.indexOf("b"), cIdx = order1.indexOf("c");
console.assert(aIdx < bIdx, "a before b");
console.assert(bIdx < cIdx, "b before c");
console.assert(order1.includes("a") && order1.includes("b") && order1.includes("c"));

// Diamond dependency
const g2 = { a: [], b: ["a"], c: ["a"], d: ["b", "c"] };
const r2 = new DependencyResolver(g2);
const order2 = r2.resolve("d");
console.assert(order2.indexOf("a") < order2.indexOf("b"), "a before b in diamond");
console.assert(order2.indexOf("a") < order2.indexOf("c"), "a before c in diamond");
console.assert(order2.indexOf("b") < order2.indexOf("d"), "b before d in diamond");
console.assert(order2.indexOf("c") < order2.indexOf("d"), "c before d in diamond");
// No duplicates
console.assert(new Set(order2).size === order2.length, "no duplicates");

// Cycle detection
const g3 = { a: ["b"], b: ["c"], c: ["a"] };
const r3 = new DependencyResolver(g3);
try {
  r3.resolve("a");
  console.assert(false, "should throw CyclicDependencyError");
} catch (e) {
  console.assert(e instanceof CyclicDependencyError, "should be CyclicDependencyError");
  console.assert(Array.isArray(e.cycle) && e.cycle.length >= 3, "cycle path present");
}

// resolveAll
const g4 = { a: [], b: ["a"], c: ["a"], d: [] };
const r4 = new DependencyResolver(g4);
const all = r4.resolveAll();
console.assert(all.length === 4, "all 4 packages");
console.assert(all.indexOf("a") < all.indexOf("b"), "a before b in resolveAll");

console.log("ALL TESTS PASSED");
```

## Constraints
- No external packages — Node.js stdlib only
- Single file `dep_resolver.ts` or `dep_resolver.js` (ES module with named exports)

Implement PubSub class in pubsub.js as ES module. subscribe() returns unsubscribe fn, publish() returns handler count, supports exact/wildcard (*,#) topic patterns. No external packages.
Write `pubsub.ts` (or `pubsub.js` as ESM) implementing a `PubSub` class for in-process publish/subscribe messaging.

## Interface
```typescript
type Handler = (topic: string, message: unknown) => void;

class PubSub {
  subscribe(pattern: string, handler: Handler): () => void;
  // Returns an unsubscribe function.
  // Pattern can be:
  //   - exact: "user.created"
  //   - single-level wildcard: "user.*" matches "user.created", "user.deleted" but NOT "user.x.y"
  //   - multi-level wildcard: "user.#" matches "user.created", "user.profile.updated", etc.

  publish(topic: string, message: unknown): number;
  // Publish message to topic. Returns number of handlers called.

  subscriberCount(pattern: string): number;
  // Returns number of active subscriptions for this exact pattern string.
}
```

## Wildcard rules
- `*` matches exactly one topic segment (segments split by `.`)
- `#` matches zero or more segments at the end
- `user.*` matches `user.login` but not `user.login.success`
- `user.#` matches `user.login`, `user.login.success`, `user` (zero segments)
- Patterns without wildcards must match exactly

## Test assertions (must all pass)
```typescript
import { PubSub } from './pubsub.js';

const ps = new PubSub();
const received: Array<[string, unknown]> = [];

// Exact subscription
const unsub1 = ps.subscribe("user.login", (t, m) => received.push([t, m]));
ps.publish("user.login", { userId: 1 });
ps.publish("user.logout", { userId: 1 }); // no match

// Wildcard *
const unsub2 = ps.subscribe("order.*", (t, m) => received.push([t, m]));
ps.publish("order.created", { id: 42 });
ps.publish("order.updated", { id: 42 });
ps.publish("order.item.added", { id: 42 }); // no match for order.*

// Wildcard #
const unsub3 = ps.subscribe("log.#", (t, m) => received.push([t, m]));
ps.publish("log.error", "crash");
ps.publish("log.db.query.slow", "slow query");

console.assert(received.length === 5, `expected 5 messages, got ${received.length}`);

// Unsubscribe
unsub1();
received.length = 0;
ps.publish("user.login", "x");
console.assert(received.length === 0, "should not receive after unsubscribe");

// subscriberCount
console.assert(ps.subscriberCount("order.*") === 1);
console.assert(ps.subscriberCount("log.#") === 1);
unsub2();
console.assert(ps.subscriberCount("order.*") === 0);

// publish returns count
unsub3();
const count = ps.publish("any.topic", "hi");
console.assert(count === 0, "count should be 0 with no subscribers");

console.log("ALL TESTS PASSED");
```

## Constraints
- No external packages — Node.js stdlib only
- Single file `pubsub.ts` or `pubsub.js` (ES module with named export)
